{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q5", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0019", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:05.642+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.397, "patience_s": 17.69, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 6.9, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0019", "question": "What training cost in FLOPs does Table 2 report for the big Transformer?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 13 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 p.8 §6.1: \"On the WMT 2014 English-to-German translation task, the big transformer model (Transformer (big) in Table 2) outperforms the best previously reported models (including ensembles) by more than 2.0 BLEU, establishing a new state-of-the-art BLEU score of 28.4. The configuration of this model is listed in the bottom line of Table 3. Training took 3.5 days on 8 P100 GPUs. Even our base model surpasses all previously published models and ensembles, at a fraction of the training cost of any of the competitive models.\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 pp.7-8 §5.4: \"Model BLEU Training Cost (FLOPs) EN-DE EN-FR EN-DE EN-FR ByteNet [18] 23.75 Deep-Att + PosUnk [39] 39.2 1.0 · 10^20 GNMT + RL [38] 24.6 39.92 2.3 · 10^19 1.4 · 10^20 ConvS2S [9] 25.16 40.46 9.6 · 10^18 1.5 · 10^20 MoE [32] 26.03 40.56 2.0 · 10^19 1.2 · 10^20 Deep-Att + PosUnk Ensemble [39] 40.4 8.0 · 10^20 GNMT + RL Ensemble [38] 26.30 41.16 1.8 · 10^20 1.1 · 10^21 ConvS2S Ensemble [9] 26.36 41.29 7.7 · 10^19 1.2 · 10^21 Transformer (base model) 27.3 38.1 3.3 · 10^18 Transformer (big) 28.4 41.8 2.3 · 10^19 Residual Dropout We apply dropout [33] to the output of each sub-layer, before it is add\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.8 §6.1", "pid": "attention:2400:0019", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p08-b007", "attention-p08-b008", "attention-p08-b009", "attention-p08-b010"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.7-8 §5.4", "pid": "attention:2400:0018", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p07-b015", "attention-p08-b001", "attention-p08-b002", "attention-p08-b003", "attention-p08-b004"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 13.0, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 22.436202299999422, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 13.76655480000045, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.73500000000058}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 21.663702300000296, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 12.979054800001904, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.73500000000058}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 1.1, "end_ms": 1.7, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What training cost in FLOPs does Table 2 report for the big Transformer?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.866, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 17.458, "query_terms": 8}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.2, "end_ms": 3.2, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.2, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q7", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0020", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:05.642+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.408, "patience_s": 19.95, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 7.0, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0020", "question": "Why do the authors say traditional spoken dialogue systems struggle to feel natural?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 15 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 p.2 §1: \"A solution to this challenge is handling the NLU and NLG with an LLM, while the ASR and TTS provide the voice interface during the user’s and the system’s turn respectively (Llama, 2024). This framework supports the current generation of spoken dialogue systems such as Gemini (Gemini et al., 2023) or ChatGPT.^4 Yet, the experience offered by these interfaces remains far from natural conversations. First, latency compounds along the many components of these pipelines, resulting in a typical global latency of several seconds.\"\n[S2] 2410.00037v1 p.1 Abstract: \"We introduce Moshi, a speech-text foundation model and full-duplex spoken dialogue framework. Current systems for spoken dialogue rely on pipelines of independent components, namely voice activity detection, speech recognition, textual dialogue and text-to-speech. Such frameworks cannot emulate the experience of real conversations. First, their complexity induces a latency of several seconds between interactions. Second, text being the intermediate modality for dialogue, non-linguistic information that modifies meaning— such as emotion or non-speech sounds— is lost in the interaction.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.2 §1", "pid": "moshi:2400:0001", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p02-b002", "moshi-p02-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2410.00037v1 p.1 Abstract", "pid": "moshi:2400:0000", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p01-b005"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 14.9, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.7, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 24.573702300003788, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 15.93405480000365, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.73500000000058}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 23.546202300000004, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 14.89155480000045, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.73500000000058}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.7, "end_ms": 0.9, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "Why do the authors say traditional spoken dialogue systems struggle to feel natural?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.426, "union": 0.426, "top_bm25": 14.626, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.2, "end_ms": 3.2, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.2, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q13", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0004", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:02.201+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 20.951, "patience_s": 18.59, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3495.3, "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0004", "question": "In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "The encoder network takes the raw input sequence and processes it at the fine‑grained (original) resolution before any down‑sampling occurs, while the decoder network receives the up‑sampled representations and operates again at the original resolution to reconstruct the output sequence [S2][S3].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.19-20 §3.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0035", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p19-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p20-b001"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.2 §1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0003", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p02-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p02-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p02-b010"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.4-5 §2.1.1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0007", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p04-b013", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b014", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b015", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b002", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b003", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b004", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b009"]}], "notice": "checked for citations only (no second-model check)", "eta_s": null, "model_calls": [{"step": "understand", "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 239, "completion_tokens": 50}, {"step": "draft", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 1807, "completion_tokens": 103}], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.6, "model": null, "outcome": "full", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 0.0, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 0.0, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 0.0}}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b"}}, {"name": "understand", "start_ms": 0.6, "end_ms": 464.5, "model": null, "outcome": "router", "detail": {}}, {"name": "wait:understand", "start_ms": 0.6, "end_ms": 0.6, "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "outcome": "granted", "detail": {"waited_s": 0.0}}, {"name": "call:understand", "start_ms": 0.6, "end_ms": 464.5, "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "outcome": "200:ok", "detail": {}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 464.5, "end_ms": 464.9, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "encoder decoder hierarchical resolution token sequence In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "papers": ["dynamic_chunking"], "k": 3, "evidence": {"best": 0.728, "union": 0.728, "top_bm25": 23.337, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "decide", "start_ms": 464.9, "end_ms": 465.0, "model": null, "outcome": "draft", "detail": {"best": 0.728, "union": 0.728, "top_bm25": 23.337, "query_terms": 7}}, {"name": "draft", "start_ms": 465.1, "end_ms": 3493.2, "model": null, "outcome": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "detail": {"level": "full"}}, {"name": "wait:draft", "start_ms": 465.1, "end_ms": 465.2, "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "outcome": "granted", "detail": {"waited_s": 0.0}}, {"name": "call:draft", "start_ms": 465.2, "end_ms": 3493.2, "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "outcome": "200:ok", "detail": {}}, {"name": "check", "start_ms": 3493.2, "end_ms": 3494.1, "model": null, "outcome": "pass", "detail": {"gate_ok": true, "failures": 0, "verifier": null}}, {"name": "verify", "start_ms": 3493.9, "end_ms": 3494.1, "model": null, "outcome": "skipped (no headroom)", "detail": {}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3494.1, "end_ms": 3494.1, "model": null, "outcome": "answer", "detail": {}}], "checks": {"gate": {"ok": true, "sentences": 1, "failures": []}, "verifier": null, "draft_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "attempts": 1, "router": {"papers": ["dynamic_chunking"], "query": "encoder decoder hierarchical resolution token sequence In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "alt_query": "compression upscaling chunking hierarchy neural network stages In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?"}, "retrieval": {"query": "encoder decoder hierarchical resolution token sequence In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "papers": ["dynamic_chunking"], "k": 3, "pids": ["dynamic_chunking:2400:0035", "dynamic_chunking:2400:0003", "dynamic_chunking:2400:0007"]}}, "released_ms": 3494.1, "budget_wait_ms": 0.1, "tokens_in": 2046, "tokens_out": 153, "level_name": "full", "pipeline_model_calls": [{"step": "understand", "model": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 239, "completion_tokens": 50, "tokens_estimated": false, "ms": 463.62740000040503, "waited_s": 0.0}, {"step": "draft", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 1807, "completion_tokens": 103, "tokens_estimated": false, "ms": 3027.66540000448, "waited_s": 0.0}]}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q1", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0021", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:05.815+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.563, "patience_s": 82.78, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3.0, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0021", "question": "In simple terms, what does scaled dot-product attention compute, and why divide the query-key dot products by sqrt(d_k)?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 11 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 p.4 §3.2.1: \"We call our particular attention \"Scaled Dot-Product Attention\" (Figure 2). The input consists of queries and keys of dimension d_k, and values of dimension d_v. We compute the dot products of the query with all keys, divide each by √ d_k, and apply a softmax function to obtain the weights on the values. In practice, we compute the attention function on a set of queries simultaneously, packed together into a matrix Q. The keys and values are also packed together into matrices K and V .\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2: \"An attention function can be described as mapping a query and a set of key-value pairs to an output, where the query, keys, values, and output are all vectors. The output is computed as a weighted sum Scaled Dot-Product Attention Multi-Head Attention Figure 2: (left) Scaled Dot-Product Attention. (right) Multi-Head Attention consists of several attention layers running in parallel. of the values, where the weight assigned to each value is computed by a compatibility function of the query with the corresponding key.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.4 §3.2.1", "pid": "attention:2400:0006", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p04-b005", "attention-p04-b006", "attention-p04-b007", "attention-p04-b008", "attention-p04-b009", "attention-p04-b010", "attention-p04-b011", "attention-p04-b012", "attention-p04-b013"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0005", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p03-b007", "attention-p04-b001", "attention-p04-b002", "attention-p04-b003"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 11.4, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 14.381702300001052, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 13.939554800002952, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.56300000000192}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 11.831702300005418, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 11.352054800001497, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.56300000000192}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 1.1, "end_ms": 1.7, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "In simple terms, what does scaled dot-product attention compute, and why divide the query-key dot products by sqrt(d_k)?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.832, "union": 0.832, "top_bm25": 54.238, "query_terms": 13}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 2.9, "end_ms": 2.9, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 2.9, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q6", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0022", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:05.975+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.722, "patience_s": 19.87, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 7.1, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0022", "question": "What was the Transformer's 95th percentile inference latency on the WMT 2014 English-German test set?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 12 s.\n[S1] 1706.03762v7 p.7 §5.1: \"We trained on the standard WMT 2014 English-German dataset consisting of about 4.5 million sentence pairs. Sentences were encoded using byte-pair encoding [3], which has a shared source-target vocabulary of about 37000 tokens. For English-French, we used the significantly larger WMT 2014 English-French dataset consisting of 36M sentences and split tokens into a 32000 word-piece vocabulary [38]. Sentence pairs were batched together by approximate sequence length. Each training batch contained a set of sentence pairs containing approximately 25000 source tokens and 25000 target tokens.\"\n[S2] 1706.03762v7 p.8 §6.1: \"On the WMT 2014 English-to-German translation task, the big transformer model (Transformer (big) in Table 2) outperforms the best previously reported models (including ensembles) by more than 2.0 BLEU, establishing a new state-of-the-art BLEU score of 28.4. The configuration of this model is listed in the bottom line of Table 3. Training took 3.5 days on 8 P100 GPUs. Even our base model surpasses all previously published models and ensembles, at a fraction of the training cost of any of the competitive models.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.7 §5.1", "pid": "attention:2400:0015", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p07-b007"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.8 §6.1", "pid": "attention:2400:0019", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p08-b007", "attention-p08-b008", "attention-p08-b009", "attention-p08-b010"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 11.6, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 1.3, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 14.173202300000412, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 13.73855480000202, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.40699999999924}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 12.028202299996337, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 11.556054799999401, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.40699999999924}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 1.3, "end_ms": 1.9, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What was the Transformer's 95th percentile inference latency on the WMT 2014 English-German test set?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.708, "union": 0.708, "top_bm25": 22.335, "query_terms": 12}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.4, "end_ms": 3.4, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.4, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q14", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0023", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:05.975+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.727, "patience_s": 30.6, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 7.2, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0023", "question": "How does H-Net decide where to cut the byte stream into pieces?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 13 s.\n[S1] 2507.07955v2 pp.14-15 §3.1: \"Visualization of Tokenized Positions. In Figure 4, we provide visualizations of the boundaries dynamically drawn by H- Net (1-stage) and H-Net (2-stage). The visualization offers several insights about how the model decides boundaries. • Single-stage behavior: H-Net (1-stage) predominantly places boundaries at whitespace characters, closely mirroring the delimiters used by SpaceByte. This indicates that H-Net learns that word boundaries represent natural semantic units in text.\"\n[S2] 2507.07955v2 p.10 §2.4: \"(c) Step the main network if necessary, in which case we also need to step the dechunking layer. (d) Use the result of the dechunking layer to step the decoder network. A consequence of this inference formulation is that, at inference time, H-Net decides individually for each token how much compute to use when processing it. Therefore, H-Net can allocate more or less compute to different tokens as it deems necessary. A particular connection is that inference resembles speculative decoding (Leviathan, Kalman, and Matias 2023; Chen et al.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.14-15 §3.1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0028", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p14-b004", "dynamic_chunking-p14-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p14-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p14-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p14-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p15-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p15-b002", "dynamic_chunking-p15-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.10 §2.4", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0019", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p10-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p10-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p10-b010"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 12.9, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.8, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 14.45070229999692, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 14.016054799998528, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.40699999999924}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 13.355702300003031, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 12.906054799997946, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.40699999999924}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.8, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "How does H-Net decide where to cut the byte stream into pieces?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.595, "union": 0.595, "top_bm25": 10.362, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.3, "end_ms": 3.3, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.3, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q8", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0024", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:06.095+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.851, "patience_s": 42.86, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3.4, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0024", "question": "What are Moshi's main components and what does each do?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 15 s.\n[S1] 2410.00037v1 pp.6-7 §3.1: \"To jointly model the audio streams from Moshi and the user, as well as Moshi’s text tokens, we rely on a Depth Transformer compatible with streaming inference (Sections 3.4.1, 3.4.2). In this section, we further detail each of these components. We then describe the training datasets and the different training phases we used to train Moshi in Section 4. Finally, in Section 5, we report thorough evaluation results on Moshi’s abilities, both linguistic and acoustic, as well as ablation experiments on its main components, while Section 6 provides analyses on the safety of our system.\"\n[S2] 2507.07955v2 pp.4-5 §2.1.1: \"H-Net employs a hierarchical architecture comprising three primary components – encoder networks (E), main network (M), and decoder networks (D) – where each component is implemented with a stack of sequence mixing layers (e.g., Transformers or state space models). In its simplest form, a single-stage H-Net consists of one encoder network, one main network, and one decoder network. Crucially, the architecture’s key characteristic lies in the main network’s unique property: it can itself be instantiated as a complete H-Net, enabling recursive construction of multi-level hierarchies.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2410.00037v1 pp.6-7 §3.1", "pid": "moshi:2400:0009", "paper": "moshi", "block_ids": ["moshi-p06-b005", "moshi-p07-b001", "moshi-p07-b002"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.4-5 §2.1.1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0007", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p04-b013", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b014", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b015", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b002", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b003", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b004", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b009"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 14.8, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.9, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 18.848202300003322, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 18.481054800002312, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.28199999999924}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 15.18070230000012, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 14.761054800001148, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.28199999999924}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.9, "end_ms": 1.3, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "What are Moshi's main components and what does each do?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 8.984, "query_terms": 5}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 3.4, "end_ms": 3.4, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 3.4, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q13", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0025", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:06.175+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 24.932, "patience_s": 89.72, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 4.4, "degraded": true, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0025", "question": "In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "kind": "passages", "level": 3, "text": "No written answer this time: here are the most relevant passages from the papers, quoted as they are. A written answer should be possible in about 16 s.\n[S1] 2507.07955v2 pp.19-20 §3.3: \"A natural question arises: does the importance of Mamba layers (i) stem specifically from processing fine-grained byte inputs, or (ii) because they are better for compressing information into the next stage, even at coarser input resolutions? To investigate these hypotheses, we train a 1-stage H-Net on top of BPE-tokenized inputs processed by the GPT-2 tokenizer. We then evaluate six different encoder-decoder combinations.\"\n[S2] 2507.07955v2 pp.4-5 §2.1.1: \"H-Net employs a hierarchical architecture comprising three primary components – encoder networks (E), main network (M), and decoder networks (D) – where each component is implemented with a stack of sequence mixing layers (e.g., Transformers or state space models). In its simplest form, a single-stage H-Net consists of one encoder network, one main network, and one decoder network. Crucially, the architecture’s key characteristic lies in the main network’s unique property: it can itself be instantiated as a complete H-Net, enabling recursive construction of multi-level hierarchies.\"", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.19-20 §3.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0035", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p19-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p19-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p20-b001"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 pp.4-5 §2.1.1", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:2400:0007", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p04-b013", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b014", "dynamic_chunking-p04-b015", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b001", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b002", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b003", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b004", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b005", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p05-b009"]}], "notice": "passages only, no model call: no drafter free within 8 s", "eta_s": 15.5, "model_calls": [], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 1.1, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 20.03770230000373, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 19.693054800001846, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.20400000000518}, "lean": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 15.860202300005767, "openai/gpt-oss-20b": 15.455554800006212, "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": 56.20400000000518}}, "drafter": null}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 1.1, "end_ms": 1.8, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "In H-Net, what do the encoder and decoder networks do, and at what resolution do they run?", "papers": null, "k": 2, "evidence": {"best": 0.728, "union": 0.728, "top_bm25": 13.078, "query_terms": 7}}}, {"name": "send", "start_ms": 4.3, "end_ms": 4.3, "model": null, "outcome": "passages", "detail": {}}], "checks": {}, "released_ms": 4.3, "budget_wait_ms": 0, "tokens_in": 0, "tokens_out": 0, "level_name": "passages", "pipeline_model_calls": []}}
{"type": "answer", "run_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "burst", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q2", "request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0012", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:32:03.695+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 22.443, "patience_s": 38.52, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 3135.3, "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "burst-20261110T183141Z-0012", "question": "Why use several attention heads instead of one, and what configuration did the paper use?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "An attention function can be described as mapping a query and a set of key-value pairs to an output, where the query, keys, values, and output are all vectors. [S1]", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0005", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p03-b007", "attention-p04-b001", "attention-p04-b002", "attention-p04-b003"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.4-5 §3.2.2", "pid": "attention:2400:0007", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p04-b015", "attention-p04-b016", "attention-p04-b017", "attention-p05-b001", "attention-p05-b002", "attention-p05-b003", "attention-p05-b004", "attention-p05-b005", "attention-p05-b006"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.13-15 Attention Visualizations (appendix)", "pid": "attention:2400:0023", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p13-b002", "attention-p14-b001", "attention-p15-b001"]}], "notice": "the question was searched as written (query rewriting was skipped); checked for citations only (no second-model check)", "eta_s": null, "model_calls": [{"step": "draft", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 911, "completion_tokens": 61}], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.6, "model": null, "outcome": "full", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/g